14	// The preview pane, dressed as a browser: an address row that says what you
15	// are looking at, a width switcher, zoom, refresh, open-in-a-real-tab and
16	// fullscreen.
17	//
18	// THE PARTS THAT MUST NOT CHANGE, and why (all verified in preview-agent.ts,
19	// which has the measurements):
20	//
21	//  * The iframe is fed a SELF-CONTAINED document through `srcdoc`, never
22	//    pointed at a URL. Every subresource request made from inside this frame
23	//    is treated as cross-site and arrives without the session cookie, so a
24	//    preview assembled out of separate asset requests cannot load at all.
25	//  * `sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms"` with NO `allow-same-origin`.
26	//    Adding it would hand generated customer code our own origin.
27	//  * The iframe element is not re-keyed when the width or the zoom changes, so
28	//    those resize the live page instead of reloading it. That is the
29	//    difference between testing a responsive layout and testing a page load.
30	//    Only `reloadKey` re-keys it, which is what Refresh bumps.
31	//
32	// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
33	//
34	//  * No rounded phone shell and no notch. A device chrome would imply we are
35	//    emulating a device; we are changing a width, and the pixel number next to
36	//    the label is the thing worth trusting.
37	//  * No "test your form" affordance. Forms in the DRAFT preview do nothing —
38	//    submissions are only wired for published sites (lib/site-forms.ts), and
39	//    app/api/preview/…/route.ts says so at length. A button that appeared to
40	//    test one would discard the enquiry it was pretending to send.
41	//  * The address row is not an input. There is nothing to navigate to: the
42	//    assembled document is one page with no URL of its own (it runs from
43	//    about:srcdoc), so a text field you could type into would be a control
44	//    that cannot do the only thing an address bar does.
